{
  // Pinned devbox GLOBAL manifest for the default worker toolchain (PRD #83 M1, Q2).
  //
  // go + python3 + pip are the owner's "the default worker should have docker/python/go
  // available" set. They are delivered through the ONE nix/devbox provisioning path
  // (PRD #18 — no second apk package path, no version drift), realized into the baked
  // image /nix at BUILD time via `devbox global install` so first use is instant instead
  // of paying the cold-start closure fetch. Each template Dockerfile copies this file
  // (agent/devbox-global/devbox.json) into devbox's global config dir and installs it
  // (base + jvm, in lockstep — jvm is NOT `FROM base`). It lives OUTSIDE agent/templates/
  // because that dir is one-directory-per-worker-image; this is shared build config, not
  // a template.
  //
  // devbox.json is parsed as HuJSON, so these `//` comments are valid.
  //
  // NOTE (pinning): the sibling `devbox.lock` pins the nixpkgs revision to an exact
  // COMMIT (generated by the tester's live arm64 build), so go/python/pip do
  // NOT drift per rebuild. Each Dockerfile COPYs the lock in BEFORE `devbox global install`
  // — without that COPY the image never sees it and the pin would float. Bump both the
  // manifest and the lock together when upgrading the toolchain.
  //
  // CORRECTION (2026-07-25, PRD #123): the line above read "an exact resolved store path".
  // It does not. Every entry in devbox.lock resolves to a FLAKE REF —
  // `github:NixOS/nixpkgs/20535e48…#<attr>` — not a `/nix/store/…` path; there is no
  // store_path key in the file at all. The distinction is load-bearing, not pedantic: a
  // store path would need no evaluation and therefore no github, whereas a flake ref means
  // nix must still evaluate nixpkgs, which is why PRD #123 treats "just commit a lockfile"
  // as an unproven spike rather than the obvious fix. The pin against DRIFT is real; the
  // pin against NETWORK is not what this file provides.
  //
  // `pip` is nixpkgs `python3Packages.pip` (there is no top-level `pip` attr); the tester
  // confirms `pip`/`python3`/`go` all resolve on the agent PATH of the built image (Q-C).
  //
  // `gcc` completes the go/python3 toolchains rather than being a repo-specific extra:
  // `go test -race` and cgo need a C compiler/linker (this repo's own store integration
  // tests run `-race`), and python C-extension wheels need one too. Delivered through the
  // SAME nix/devbox path as go so cgo links against the matching toolchain (an apk/musl
  // gcc would mismatch nix-glibc go). Validate `cgo`/`-race` in the built image.
  //
  // PRD #87 — the browser for the `web-ux` role rides this SAME shared toolchain (the
  // owner's chosen delivery), so it lands in every worker image (base + jvm) with one
  // manifest edit and no dedicated image variant:
  //   * `chromium` — the headless browser agent-browser drives (mainProgram `chromium`,
  //     so it resolves at /opt/uzi-toolchain/bin/chromium; AGENT_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE_PATH
  //     in both Dockerfiles points there). Prebuilt in cache.nixos.org on x86_64-linux at
  //     the pinned rev (chromium-150.0.7871.128, narinfo HTTP 200), so CI's amd64-only
  //     kaniko build substitutes it rather than compiling Chromium from source.
  //   * `fontconfig` + `dejavu_fonts` — a nix Chromium closure ships NO fonts, so without
  //     a font package + a fontconfig that can find it every screenshot (the role's whole
  //     evidence artifact) renders tofu. Both prebuilt at the pinned rev. The Dockerfiles
  //     wire FONTCONFIG_FILE at /opt/uzi-toolchain/share/fonts and pre-warm the cache.
  // agent-browser itself is NOT a nix package — it is the pinned npm CLI in
  // agent/package.json (baked by `npm ci`), never installed per-run.
  //
  // `openssl` is a broadly-useful base crypto/TLS CLI (cert generation, `openssl rand`,
  // hashing, PEM/DER) that many repos' test harnesses and scripts reach for — the same
  // "every worker should have it" class as go/python3, NOT a repo-specific extra like a
  // Homebrew formula's ruby. It was first surfaced by uzi's own e2e harness
  // (e2e/run-e2e.sh forge-fake self-signed cert; judge rec, run dd06c0ed) but lives here
  // in the baked toolchain rather than as tier-2 so its absence never surfaces a silent
  // exit 127 to an agent in ANY repo. Its closure is small and largely already present
  // via the other packages' deps, so it adds negligible image weight.
  //
  // NOTE: it MUST be `openssl.bin`, not bare `openssl`. nixpkgs splits openssl into
  // outputs and its outputsToInstall is [bin, man] with NO `out`, so `devbox global
  // install` of bare `openssl` lands only libs/man and `command -v openssl` 127s — the
  // build guard then fails (proven: MR !103 pipeline 19969, build:agent). The `.bin`
  // output carries the `openssl` CLI; verified `devbox install` at the pinned rev puts
  // /opt/uzi-toolchain/bin/openssl on PATH. (gcc/go/python3 don't hit this — their CLI
  // is in `out`, which IS installed.)
  //
  // `file`, `perl` and `coreutils` (2026-07-27) close the three open `install_worker_tool`
  // judge recommendations that are genuinely missing toolchain. Sweep: all 21 runs via
  // `uzi review backlog --bucket all` (not truncated), 14 tool groups, 8 still open. Same
  // "every worker should have it" class as openssl, so they live here, not as tier-2:
  //   * `file` — artifact validation, asked for by two runs (b64b98f3, 1dfc65b4). The
  //     fact-checker hex-dumped a PNG header to read magic bytes + IHDR because it had no
  //     `file`; the lead had to infer shim-vs-real-binary from `head` output. Browser/UX
  //     verification runs keep needing it.
  //   * `perl` — one-shot in-place edits (run bedfc75d). A reviewer's mutation sweep hit
  //     exit 127 on `perl -0pi -e` and recovered via a python3 heredoc. Near-universal
  //     assumption; cheap to stop being a surprise.
  //   * `coreutils` — for `fmt` (run c13cff61: two hand-rewrap round-trips on hard-wrapped
  //     docs under a line-count build gate). nixpkgs ships no standalone `fmt`; GNU
  //     coreutils is the only source of it.
  //
  // COST OF `coreutils`, measured and accepted (owner decision, 2026-07-27). This dir's
  // profile is FIRST on the agent PATH (/opt/uzi-toolchain/bin), so GNU coreutils SHADOWS
  // 82 busybox applets — ls, cp, mv, date, sort, stat, head, tail, wc, printf, du, df, … —
  // for every run in every repo, not just `fmt`. Measured by diffing the built agent
  // image's /bin + /usr/bin against the coreutils `bin` dir at the pinned rev. GNU is
  // broadly a superset of busybox but NOT identical (`sort`, `date`, `stat` formats
  // differ), so a script that silently relied on busybox behaviour can change. If odd
  // shell breakage appears after this rolls, `coreutils` is the first suspect and the
  // cheapest of the three to drop — `fmt` is the least valuable of them.
  //
  // `file` MUST be `file.out`, for the same reason openssl must be `openssl.bin` — and this
  // one was learned the expensive way, so read it before adding any multi-output package.
  //
  // CORRECTION (2026-07-27, same day): this block previously read "meta.outputsToInstall is
  // ["out","man"] / ["out","man"] / ["out"] — all three carry `out`, so they do NOT hit the
  // openssl split-output trap above and stay bare attrs." The premise is true and the
  // CONCLUSION IS FALSE. `meta.outputsToInstall` is a nixpkgs attribute; it is NOT what
  // devbox's resolver uses. Measured: with a bare `file`, `devbox global install` exits 0
  // and the realized profile manifest carries `file-5.48-DEV` — the headers/libmagic
  // output, which contains no `bin/file` at all. The profile therefore has no `file`, and
  // `command -v file` 127s in the image.
  //
  // 0.11.11 SHIPPED `file` BROKEN because of this. Two things hid it: `devbox global
  // install` reports success, and a project-scope `devbox install` + `devbox run` (how it
  // was first "verified") puts the raw /nix/store path on PATH, so `file` resolved there
  // while being absent from the profile the image actually uses. The tell was visible and
  // missed — the other four resolved under `.devbox/nix/profile/default/bin` and `file`
  // resolved under `/nix/store/...`. Verify a package against the PROFILE
  // (`readlink -f .devbox/nix/profile/default` then `ls bin/`), never against `command -v`
  // inside `devbox run`.
  //
  // Caught by the build guard's first real run (agent/devbox-global/assert-toolchain.sh),
  // which is the entire argument for that guard existing.
  //
  // Verified at the pinned rev (x86_64-linux eval + live arm64-linux `devbox global
  // install` into the profile, 2026-07-27):
  //   * `file.out`, `perl`, `coreutils`, `kubernetes-helm` and `kubeconform` all land
  //     bin/file, bin/perl, bin/fmt, bin/helm and bin/kubeconform IN THE PROFILE.
  //   * exercised from that profile: `file --version` → file-5.48, `perl -e` runs,
  //     `fmt -w 20` rewraps, `helm version --short` → v4.2.3, `kubeconform -v` → v0.7.0.
  //   * all narinfos are HTTP 200 on cache.nixos.org, so CI's amd64-only kaniko build
  //     substitutes them rather than compiling.
  //
  // NOTE (as load-bearing as the openssl one): write them BARE, never `file@latest`. A
  // plain `devbox add file perl coreutils` resolves through devbox-search, which pins each
  // package to ITS OWN nixpkgs revision (measured: file/perl → 7525d999…, coreutils →
  // 6d120041…) and writes a fat `systems:` block — dragging two EXTRA nixpkgs revisions
  // into an image whose whole point is one pinned rev. Bare attrs resolve against the
  // manifest's own nixpkgs input and lock as `source: "nixpkg"` at 20535e48…, matching
  // every entry above. Add packages by editing this file + `devbox install`, not `devbox add`.
  //
  // `kubernetes-helm` + `kubeconform` (2026-07-27, owner decision) give an agent editing
  // deploy/chart/ a LOCAL gate. uzi ships a 2809-line chart whose only gate today is CI's
  // `helm_chart` job (`helm lint` plus four `helm template` runs
  // with invariant guards), so a chart edit is committed blind and fails minutes later in
  // CI. `helm template … | kubeconform -strict` reproduces most of that locally:
  //   * `kubernetes-helm` — `helm lint` / `helm template` / `helm dependency`. The attr is
  //     `kubernetes-helm`; the binary it puts on PATH is `helm` (mainProgram).
  //   * `kubeconform` — offline SCHEMA validation of rendered manifests, the half kubectl
  //     cannot do without a cluster (`--dry-run=client` is parse-level only;
  //     `--dry-run=server` needs a live API server).
  //
  // KNOWN AND ACCEPTED (owner decision, 2026-07-27): this pinned rev carries helm **4.2.3**
  // and has no helm-3 attr, while CI pins **3.16.4** (the `helm_chart` job's
  // `alpine/helm:3.16.4` pin — that string appears twice in .gitlab-ci.yml, the
  // other being `publish:chart`'s, so "the helm_chart job's pin" is the
  // unambiguous form).
  // A local helm run is therefore a SMOKE CHECK, NOT a mirror of the CI gate: a chart edit
  // that is green locally can still fail `helm_chart`, and the 3→4 breaking-change set has
  // NOT been enumerated here. CI's helm 3 stays the authority — an agent must not reshape
  // the chart to satisfy local helm 4 against a red CI job. Second gap: the CI job runs
  // `helm repo add cnpg` + `helm dependency build`, so any local render needing the CNPG
  // subchart also needs worker egress to the CNPG chart repo.
  //
  // Verified at the pinned rev (2026-07-27): both are single-output (meta.outputsToInstall
  // == ["out"]), so neither hits the openssl split-output trap above; mainPrograms `helm`
  // and `kubeconform`; both narinfos are HTTP 200 on cache.nixos.org for x86_64-linux
  // (79 MiB and 9.1 MiB), so the amd64 kaniko build substitutes rather than compiles.
  //
  // `go-task` + `jq` (2026-08-07) close two more open `install_worker_tool` judge recs,
  // both surfaced by the deterministic pre-scan on run 5bf8edb3 reporting them not-found
  // on the worker. Same "every worker should have it" class as openssl/file/perl/coreutils,
  // NOT a repo-specific extra like the Homebrew formula's `ruby` (which stays tier-2 in the
  // root devbox.json), so they live here in the baked toolchain:
  //   * `go-task` — closes a local-vs-CI gate-FIDELITY gap. This repo drives its canonical
  //     gates through `Taskfile.yml` (`task gate:*`); a worker without `task` cannot exercise
  //     those recipes and falls back to invoking `go build` / `tsc` / `node --test` by hand,
  //     a worse mirror of what CI runs. The attr is `go-task`; its mainProgram is `task`, so
  //     it puts bin/task on PATH. Single-output Go build, so the bare attr is correct (no
  //     `.bin`/`.out` suffix). (This is the BAKED global manifest; the root CLAUDE.md note
  //     that `task` "does NOT go in devbox.json" is about the repo's TIER-2 root devbox.json,
  //     a different file with a different purpose — no contradiction.)
  //   * `jq` — near-universal JSON CLI reached for across scripts. Requested as `jq.bin`, not
  //     bare `jq`, the same defensive split-output attr as `openssl.bin` / `file.out`: jq is a
  //     multi-output nixpkgs package (measured at this rev: outputs bin/doc/man/dev/out) and its
  //     CLI lives in `bin`, so `jq.bin` names the CLI-bearing output unambiguously. NOTE this is
  //     a milder case than `file`: at this pinned rev `jq.meta.outputsToInstall` == [bin man], so
  //     a BARE `jq` would in fact still land bin/jq — jq does NOT reproduce the file-broke-in-
  //     0.11.11 failure here (that was `file`, whose outputsToInstall excluded its CLI output).
  //     `jq.bin` is chosen anyway because it is explicit and stays correct if outputsToInstall
  //     ever changes. Whichever attr, verify against the PROFILE (readlink -f the profile then
  //     `ls bin/`), never `command -v` inside `devbox run` — the check that would have caught
  //     the `file` regression.
  // Both written BARE (never `go-task@latest` / `jq.bin@latest`) for the pinning reason the
  // openssl/file note above records — a versioned/`devbox add` form pins each to its own
  // nixpkgs rev; bare attrs lock as `source: "nixpkg"` at 20535e48…, matching every entry.
  //
  // `shellcheck.bin` (2026-08-07) closes the open `install_worker_tool` judge rec for
  // `shellcheck` (run 84b6a933, review 4d950db6). Same "every worker should have it" class as
  // openssl/file/perl/coreutils/go-task/jq — a general shell linter, NOT a repo-specific extra
  // like the root devbox.json's `ruby@3.3` (which is tier-2 only because uzi ships a Homebrew
  // formula). The concrete gap it fills: uzi's OWN shell-lint gate. `Taskfile.yml`'s `lint:shell`
  // (PRD #103 M5) shells out to `shellcheck` over every tracked `.sh` via `scripts/lint-shell.sh`,
  // and it is reached by `task gate:repo` — which a worker can now actually invoke because
  // `go-task` was baked in just above (2026-08-07). Without `shellcheck` on PATH that gate hits
  // `shellcheck: command not found` and `task gate:repo` exits 127.
  //
  // THE ATTR MUST BE `shellcheck.bin`, NOT bare `shellcheck` — this is the file.out trap again,
  // and it was MEASURED at the pinned rev, not assumed. nixpkgs shellcheck is multi-output
  // (outputs [bin man doc out]); its CLI lives in `bin`. A bare `shellcheck` given to devbox
  // resolves to the plain `out` output (store path …-shellcheck-0.11.0, no `-bin` suffix), which
  // ships NO `bin/shellcheck` at all — the realized profile ends up with no `bin` dir, exactly
  // like bare `file` did in 0.11.11. (`meta.outputsToInstall` == [bin man doc] here yet devbox
  // still lands only `out` — confirming again that outputsToInstall is NOT what devbox's resolver
  // uses.) `shellcheck.bin` names the CLI-bearing output and lands `bin/shellcheck` in the
  // profile. Verified against the PROFILE (readlink -f .devbox/nix/profile/default then `ls bin/`),
  // never `command -v` inside `devbox run` — the check that would have caught the `file` regression.
  //
  // VERSION IS LOAD-BEARING, not incidental: this rev carries shellcheck **0.11.0**, which is the
  // EXACT pin `lint:shell` asserts (`scripts/lint-shell.sh warning 0.11.0`, `exit 2` on any other
  // build — 0.10.0 does not emit SC3067 at all). So baking THIS build makes `task gate:repo` pass;
  // baking any other shellcheck would turn the current exit 127 into a hard exit 2 (a broken
  // instrument), which is worse, not better. If a future rev bump moves shellcheck off 0.11.0,
  // this attr and the `lint:shell` pin must move together.
  //
  // Written BARE of any version/`@latest` suffix (the `.bin` is an OUTPUT selector, not a version)
  // for the pinning reason recorded above; locks as `source: "nixpkg"` at 20535e48…. Verified at
  // the pinned rev (2026-08-07): `shellcheck.bin` lands bin/shellcheck in the profile via live
  // `devbox install`, `shellcheck --version` → 0.11.0; its bin-output narinfo
  // (ixvpvkr2s92pbi6si1ki8yk3fs1gvlf2) is HTTP 200 on cache.nixos.org for x86_64-linux (1.5 MiB
  // download / 7.5 MiB unpacked), so CI's amd64-only kaniko build SUBSTITUTES it rather than
  // compiling shellcheck (a Haskell binary) from source — the non-negotiable condition for baking it.
  //
  // `fd` + `yq-go` (2026-08-10) close two more open `install_worker_tool` judge recs
  // (run 13b3bda4-342f-4098-927d-1bb46311c64b). Same "every worker should have it, in ANY
  // repo" class as jq/file/openssl/coreutils above — general-purpose CLIs an agent reaches for
  // and 127s without, NOT a repo-specific extra like the root devbox.json's tier-2 `ruby`:
  //   * `fd` — a fast, ergonomic file finder (a `find` replacement) that scripts and agents
  //     reach for. The attr is bare `fd`; its mainProgram is `fd`, so it lands bin/fd.
  //   * `yq-go` — the STANDALONE Go YAML processor (mikefarah/yq), the `jq`-for-YAML tool.
  //     nixpkgs ships BOTH a `yq` (a thin Python jq-wrapper, `python3Packages.yq`, which drags
  //     in a python runtime) AND `yq-go`; `yq-go` is the intended tool — it is what "yq" means
  //     to almost everyone and is a single static Go binary — so it is chosen here. Its
  //     mainProgram is `yq`, so the attr `yq-go` lands bin/yq: the binary is named `yq`, NOT
  //     `yq-go` (the guard row and any smoke must call `yq`).
  // Neither hits the openssl/file split-output trap, and this was MEASURED at the pinned rev,
  // not assumed: both are SINGLE-output (`outputs` == ["out"], `meta.outputsToInstall` == ["out"]),
  // so the bare attr carries the CLI and no `.bin`/`.out` selector is needed or correct.
  // Verified at the pinned rev (2026-08-10, live x86_64-linux `devbox install` into the profile):
  // bare `fd` and `yq-go` land bin/fd and bin/yq IN THE PROFILE (readlink -f
  // .devbox/nix/profile/default then `ls bin/`, never `command -v` inside `devbox run` — the
  // check that would have caught the `file` regression); exercised from that profile,
  // `fd --version` → fd 10.4.2, `yq --version` → v4.53.3, and `yq '.a.c[1]'` over a small YAML
  // doc evaluates. Both narinfos are HTTP 200 on cache.nixos.org for x86_64-linux (fd 10.4.2
  // 0qakfy0pyvma5sv880fvhyl7n34hfhln, yq-go 4.53.3 cgad2c1jkcm04mrgf7djy5c4dyylr60v), so CI's
  // amd64-only kaniko build SUBSTITUTES them rather than compiling from source — the
  // non-negotiable condition for baking a tool in.
  // Written BARE of any version/`@latest` suffix and added by hand-editing this file +
  // `devbox install` (never `devbox add`, which pins each package to its own nixpkgs rev and
  // writes a fat `systems:` block) for the pinning reason recorded above; both lock as
  // `source: "nixpkg"` at 20535e48…, matching every entry.
  //
  // `gnumake` (2026-08-11, owner decision) bakes GNU make into every worker. Same "every
  // worker should have it, in ANY repo" class as gcc/go-task above — make is gcc's build
  // companion and countless repos drive their build/test through a Makefile, so an agent
  // without it 127s on `make`. It was first shipped (0.14.x) via the apk layer of both
  // template Dockerfiles, then MOVED here so it rides the ONE pinned nix/devbox path this
  // file's header mandates (no second apk package path, no version drift, and — unlike the
  // apk utils — covered by the build guard). The attr is bare `gnumake`; its mainProgram is
  // `make`, so it lands bin/make (the guard row is `gnumake  make  make --version`).
  //
  // gnumake IS multi-output at this rev (measured: outputs [out man info doc debug],
  // meta.outputsToInstall [out man]), but it does NOT hit the openssl.bin/file.out/
  // shellcheck.bin split-output trap, and this was MEASURED not assumed: the `make` binary
  // lives in the `out` output, so the bare attr carries the CLI and no `.bin`/`.out`
  // selector is needed or correct. Verified by substituting the x86_64-linux `out` output
  // from cache and listing it (`nix build …#legacyPackages.x86_64-linux.gnumake^out`, then
  // `ls bin/` → `make`) — the PROFILE-level check this file preaches, done cross-system
  // because the full manifest cannot `devbox install` on darwin (chromium is linux-only).
  // This rev carries make 4.4.1; its out-output narinfo (9m4gd15rxxz4xfafqysi5q7jaczxv65c)
  // is HTTP 200 on cache.nixos.org for x86_64-linux (406.8 KiB download / 1.5 MiB unpacked),
  // so CI's amd64-only kaniko build SUBSTITUTES it rather than compiling — the
  // non-negotiable condition for baking a tool in. Written BARE of any version/`@latest`
  // suffix and added by hand-editing this file + the sibling devbox.lock entry (never
  // `devbox add`, which pins to its own nixpkgs rev and writes a fat `systems:` block) for
  // the pinning reason recorded above; locks as `source: "nixpkg"` at 20535e48….
  //
  // NOT added, deliberately:
  //   * `kubectl` — weighed alongside helm and DROPPED (2026-07-27). A worker cannot reach
  //     any cluster by construction: `automountServiceAccountToken: false` at BOTH the
  //     ServiceAccount (deploy/chart/templates/worker-rbac.yaml) and the pod spec
  //     (controller/internal/kube/render.go, `automount := false`), and no kubeconfig is
  //     injected. So only client-side verbs would ever work, and they buy little here —
  //     `--dry-run=client` is parse-level, this repo has no kustomize overlays, and
  //     `helm template` + `kubeconform` already cover the ground for 57 MiB less.
  //
  // Also NOT added (both are open judge recs; neither is a package problem):
  //   * `glab` (runs b64b98f3, 1dfc65b4). The forge PAT is never in the agent env by
  //     construction — see the "PAT handling" directive in agent/src/git.ts and the
  //     scrubbed replacement env in agent/src/sdk-env.ts (buildCheckEnv) — so an installed
  //     glab would be an unauthenticated binary against a private GitLab. Giving agents
  //     forge reads is a credential-exposure design decision, not a toolchain line.
  //   * `typescript`/`vitest` (runs a6322954, bdc4c634, 95afd872) are repo-pinned dev
  //     dependencies, not toolchain; baking them would drift from whatever the clone pins.
  //     PRD #121's agent/src/js-deps.ts installs them from the repo's own lockfile.
  //   * `eslint` (open judge rec, dismissed 2026-08-07). This repo lints with OXLINT — both
  //     web/ and agent/ `package.json` `lint` scripts run `oxlint` (pinned 1.76.0) and there
  //     is no eslint anywhere in the tree. eslint is otherwise a repo-pinned dev dependency,
  //     the same class as typescript/vitest above (js-deps.ts installs those from the clone's
  //     own lockfile), so baking it globally would help nothing here.
  "packages": [
    "go",
    "gcc",
    "python3",
    "python3Packages.pip",
    "chromium",
    "fontconfig",
    "dejavu_fonts",
    "openssl.bin",
    "file.out",
    "perl",
    "coreutils",
    "kubernetes-helm",
    "kubeconform",
    "go-task",
    "jq.bin",
    "shellcheck.bin",
    "fd",
    "yq-go",
    "gnumake"
  ]
}
